[14:27:04] Hook fired. Input: {"session_id":"ea26eeef-6b8c-47d6-8920-c2a9ed971158","transcript_path":"/Users/sid-j/.claude/projects/-Users-sid-j-ai-chief-of-staff/ea26eeef-6b8c-47d6-8920-c2a9ed971158.jsonl","cwd":"/Users/sid-j/ai-chief-of-staff","permission_mode":"default","hook_event_name":"Stop","stop_hook_active":false,"last_assistant_message":"Based on the documentation, here's what likely causes authentication failures with the Google Calendar MCP:\n\n**Common causes:**\n\n1. **OAuth consent not configured** — You need to set up the OAuth consent screen in Google Cloud Console with your email as a test user. Without this, authentication is blocked.\n\n2. **Wrong credential type** — The credentials must be created as **\"Desktop app\"** type (not web application). Using the wrong type causes immediate auth failures.\n\n3. **Missing `project_id` in credentials** — The docs specifically note this can cause \"User Rate Limit Exceeded\" errors.\n\n4. **Test user not propagated** — After adding your email as a test user, wait 2-3 minutes before trying to authenticate.\n\n**How the auth flow works for this MCP server:**\n- When you first use the MCP server, it opens a browser for OAuth consent\n- After you authorize, tokens are stored locally at `~/.config/google-calendar-mcp/tokens.json`\n- If in test mode (most personal setups), tokens expire after **7 days**\n\n**To fix the current auth failure, you likely need to either:**\n1. Re-run the authentication flow (the MCP should prompt you to open a browser)\n2. Or if tokens expired, delete `~/.config/google-calendar-mcp/tokens.json` and re-authenticate\n\nWhat specific error message are you seeing?\n\nSources:\n- [CalendarMCP Authentication Docs](https://github.com/nspady/google-calendar-mcp/blob/main/docs/authentication.md)\n- [CalendarMCP](https://calendarmcp.ai/docs)"}
